<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>9.10.4: Rewriting Expressions in Vertex Form</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m02503</md:content-id>
    <md:title>9.10.4: Rewriting Expressions in Vertex Form</md:title>
    <md:uuid>d256145f-a67b-40ca-97c5-71d91548fb23</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section id="sect-00001">
      <title>Activity</title>
      <list class="os-raise-noindent" list-type="enumerated" id="list-00001">
        <item>Here is one way to rewrite \(3x^2+12x+9\) in <term class="glossary-tooltip" id="term-00001">vertex form (of a quadratic expression)</term>.</item>
      </list>
      <exercise id="exer-00001">
        <problem id="prob-00001">
          <para id="para-00001">Study the steps and write a brief explanation of what is happening at each step.</para>
          <para id="para-00002">Original expression \(3x^2+12x+9\)</para>
          <para id="para-00003"><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - \(3(x^2+4x+3)\)</para>
          <para id="para-00004"><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - \(3(x^2+4x+4+3−4)\)</para>
          <para id="para-00005"><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - \(3(x^2+4x+4−1)\)</para>
          <para id="para-00006"><emphasis>Step 4</emphasis> - \(3((x+2)^2−1)\)</para>
          <para id="para-00007"><emphasis>Step 5</emphasis> - \(3(x+2)^2−3\)</para>
          <para id="para-00008">Write down your answers, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00001">
          <para id="para-00009">Compare your answers:</para>
          <para id="para-00010">Original expression \(3x^2+12x+9\)</para>
          <para id="para-00011"><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - Rewrite the expression with 3 as a factor. \(3(x^2+4x+3)\)</para>
          <para id="para-00012"><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Add 4 to complete the square. Subtract 4 to keep the value of the expression the same. \(3(x^2+4x+4+3−4)\)</para>
          <para id="para-00013"><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Combine like terms so we can see the perfect square, \(x^2+4x+4\). \(3(x^2+4x+4−1)\)</para>
          <para id="para-00014"><emphasis>Step 4</emphasis> - Rewrite \(x^2+4x+4\) as a squared expression. \(3((x+2)^2−1)\)</para>
          <para id="para-00015"><emphasis>Step 5</emphasis> - Apply the distributive property to put the expression in vertex form. \(3(x+2)^2−3\)</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="_94449039-e496-4222-9357-adb316d75f6e" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem id="prob-00002">
          <para id="para-00016">Enter the \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex.</para>
          <para id="para-00017">What is the \(x\)-coordinate of the <term class="glossary-tooltip" id="term-00002">vertex (of a graph)</term> that represents this expression?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00002">
          <para id="para-00018">Correct! The \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex is -2.</para>
          <para id="para-00019">Nice try! The correct answer is -2. The \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex is -2.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="f18e0bfe-9bca-49e0-9491-9ba2737676f3" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem id="prob-00003">
          <para id="para-00020">Enter the \(y\)-coordinate of the vertex.</para>
          <para id="para-00021">What is the \(y\)-coordinate of the vertex of the graph that represents this expression?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00003">
          <para id="para-00022">Correct! The \(y\)-coordinate of the vertex is -3.</para>
          <para id="para-00023">Nice try! The correct answer is -3. The \(y\)-coordinate of the vertex is -3.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="exer-00002">
        <problem id="prob-00004">
          <para id="para-00024">Does the graph open upward or downward? Explain how you know.</para>
          <para id="para-00025">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00004">
          <para id="para-00026">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00027">The graph opens upward. For example, in an earlier unit, we saw that when the coefficient of the squared term \((x−h)^2\) is positive, the graph opens upward.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="exer-00003">
        <problem id="prob-00005">
          <para id="para-00028">Rewrite \(-2x^2−4x+6\) in vertex form.</para>
          <para id="para-00029">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00005">
          <para id="para-00030">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00031">\(-2(x+1)^2+8\)</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="exer-00004">
        <problem id="prob-00006">
          <para id="para-00032">Rewrite \(4x^2+24x+20\) in vertex form.</para>
          <para id="para-00033">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00006">
          <para id="para-00034">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00035">\(4(x+3)^2−16\)</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="exer-00005">
        <problem id="prob-00007">
          <para id="para-00036">Rewrite \(-x^2+20x\) in vertex form.</para>
          <para id="para-00037">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00007">
          <para id="para-00038">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00039">\(-(x−10)^2+100\)</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
    </section>
    <section id="sect-00002">
      <title>Are you ready for more?</title>
      <section id="sect-00003">
        <title>Extending Your Thinking</title>
        <exercise id="ab222ab2-5d97-49df-8acc-336fae8e8aa4" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00008">
            <para id="para-00040">Fill in the missing parts of the function \(f(x)=2(x−3)(x−9)\) in vertex form \(f(x)= \underline{\;\mathbf a\boldsymbol.\;} (x \underline{\;\mathbf b\boldsymbol.\;} \; \underline{\;\mathbf c\boldsymbol.\;} )^2 \underline{\;\mathbf d\boldsymbol.\;}\; \underline{\;\mathbf e\boldsymbol.\;} \) without completing the square. (Hint: Think about finding the zeros of the function.)</para>
            <para id="para-00041">Enter the value of a.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00008">
            <para id="para-00042">Correct! 2</para>
            <para id="para-00043">Nice try! The correct answer is 2.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_277518af-2d92-470c-ac83-5496e448abd3" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00009">
            <para id="para-00044">Enter the sign of b.</para>
            <para id="para-00045">Multiple Choice:</para>
            <para id="para-00046">Subtraction(-)</para>
            <para id="para-00047">Addition(+)</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00009">
            <para id="para-00048">Correct! Minus (-)</para>
            <para id="para-00049">Nice try! The correct answer is minus (-)</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_8cd977bb-ada8-403c-8e9c-4081c87392f6" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00010">
            <para id="para-00050">Enter the value of c.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00010">
            <para id="para-00051">Correct! 6</para>
            <para id="para-00052">Nice try! The correct answer is 6.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_910680bf-69a5-4f48-96ca-d0497eb75ee5" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00011">
            <para id="para-00053">Enter the sign of d.</para>
            <para id="para-00054">Multiple Choice:</para>
            <para id="para-00055">Subtraction(-)</para>
            <para id="para-00056">Addition(+)</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00011">
            <para id="para-00057">Correct! Minus (-)</para>
            <para id="para-00058">Nice try! The correct answer is minus (-)</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="fef2672c-3097-4539-a128-fbe4c56b1a3a" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00012">
            <para id="para-00059">Enter the value of e.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00012">
            <para id="para-00060">Correct! 18</para>
            <para id="para-00061">Nice try! The correct answer is 18.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise class="RFM1" id="exer-00006">
          <problem id="prob-00013">
            <para id="para-00062">Be prepared to show your reasoning for your answers.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00013">
            <para id="para-00063">Enter your explanation.</para>
            <para id="para-00064">Compare your answer:</para>
            <para id="para-00065">The zeros of \(f\) are 3 and 9. Because the graph of a quadratic function has a vertical line of symmetry across the vertex, the vertex of the graph must have \(x\)-coordinate 6. The \(y\)-coordinate of the vertex is -18 because \(f(6)=-18\). This means \(f(x)\) can be expressed as \(a(x−6)^2−18\). If we expand \(2(x−3)(x−9)\), we can see that the coefficient of \(x^2\) is 2. This means that \(a\) in the vertex form has to be 2 as well.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <!--End interaction-->
        <exercise id="_02de0598-1fa0-4520-ba6e-37d0ac19e22d" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00014">
            <para id="para-00066">Fill in the missing parts of the function \(g(x)=2(x−3)(x−9)+21\) in vertex form \(g(x)= \underline{\;\mathbf a\boldsymbol.\;} (x \underline{\;\mathbf b\boldsymbol.\;} \;\underline{\;\mathbf c\boldsymbol.\;} )^2 \underline{\;\mathbf d\boldsymbol.\;} \;\underline{\;\mathbf e\boldsymbol.\;}\) without completing the square.</para>
            <para id="para-00067">Enter the value of a.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00014">
            <para id="para-00068">Correct! 2</para>
            <para id="para-00069">Nice try! The correct answer is 2.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="fba80e14-fdbf-4f0d-8703-583700f83fd9" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00015">
            <para id="para-00070">Enter the sign of b.</para>
            <para id="para-00071">Multiple Choice:</para>
            <para id="para-00072">Subtraction(-)</para>
            <para id="para-00073">Addition(+)</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00015">
            <para id="para-00074">Correct! Minus (-)</para>
            <para id="para-00075">Nice try! The correct answer is minus (-)</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="d3a81070-2192-4244-b09c-b289636dca8c" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00016">
            <para id="para-00076">Enter the value of c.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00016">
            <para id="para-00077">Correct! 6</para>
            <para id="para-00078">Nice try! The correct answer is 6.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_01da4daa-5e72-4557-9a2e-2df9526aa50b" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00017">
            <para id="para-00079">Enter the sign of d.</para>
            <para id="para-00080">Multiple Choice:</para>
            <para id="para-00081">Subtraction(-)</para>
            <para id="para-00082">Addition(+)</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00017">
            <para id="para-00083">Correct! Plus (+)</para>
            <para id="para-00084">Nice try! The correct answer is plus (+)</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="e362abd6-cf29-48ab-b377-849f9629b625" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00018">
            <para id="para-00085">Enter the value of e.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00018">
            <para id="para-00086">Correct! 3</para>
            <para id="para-00087">Nice try! The correct answer is 3.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise class="RFM1" id="exer-00007">
          <problem id="prob-00019">
            <para id="para-00088">Be prepared to show your reasoning for your answers.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00019">
            <para id="para-00089">Enter your explanation.</para>
            <para id="para-00090">Compare your answer:</para>
            <para id="para-00091">Although 3 and 9 are not zeros of \(g\), when \(x\) is 3 and when \(x\) is 9, \(g(x)\) is 21, or \(g(3)=g(9)=21\). Because the graphs of quadratic functions are symmetric, the \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex is still 6. The \(y\)-coordinate of the vertex is 3 because \(g(6)=3\). The value of \(a\) is 2 for the same reason as in function \(f\).</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </section>
    </section>
    <!--End interaction-->
    <section id="sect-00004">
      <title>Video: Rewriting Expressions in Vertex Form</title>
      <para id="para-00092">Watch the following video to learn more about rewriting expressions in vertex form.</para>
